 The pinhole camera model, which simulates depth by positioning the eye behind the image plane at a distance $f$ (focal length), is the standard reference for perspective ray generation [S1, unofficial]. This approach creates an illusion of depth by casting rays through the image plane, where the ray direction is the normalized difference between the image plane point and the eye position [S1, unofficial].
 
-Historically, ray generation has evolved from simple parallel beam projections (used in early tomography) to complex perspective models. In parallel beam projections, rays are generated with identical directions, often calculated using projection angles and detector offsets [S4, unofficial]. For fan-beam projections, ray endpoints are calculated based on distance, angle, and detector offset, allowing for curved ray paths or specific geometric alignments [S4, unofficial].
